      "name": "How many days can you spend in Maine without becoming a statutory resident?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Up to 183 days in the tax year. At 184 or more days, combined with a permanent place of abode, you become a statutory resident."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What counts as a permanent place of abode in Maine?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A house, apartment, or other dwelling you maintain as your home, whether or not you own it. It does not include a seasonal camp or cottage used only for vacations, a hotel or motel room, or a student dormitory room."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do Maine's safe harbors let you avoid resident status?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. If you keep a permanent home abroad or spend only limited time in Maine, a safe harbor can let you be treated as a nonresident even when the day count would otherwise apply. The two main conditions still define the base rule."
      }
    }
  ]
}

